---
import { getEntry } from 'astro:content';
import CaseStudy from '../../layouts/CaseStudy.astro';
import Figure from '../../components/Figure.astro';
import FigurePair from '../../components/FigurePair.astro';
import DataTable from '../../components/DataTable.astro';

const entry = await getEntry('projects', 'walle');
if (!entry) throw new Error('Missing project entry: walle');
---

<CaseStudy
  entry={entry}
  standfirst="An autonomous robot that acquires colored blocks from three field zones and sorts them by color. The work worth writing up is architectural: every zone runs the same sub-machine, and a sensor characterization study replaced the entire navigation approach."
>
  <h2>Context and constraints</h2>

  <p>
    A mechatronics course project on an 8-bit-era microcontroller with a fixed
    parts kit, a fixed field, and a fixed volume budget for 3D printed material.
    The robot has to leave a base line, acquire blocks from three separate field
    zones, classify each block by color, and deliver it to the matching drop
    zone, all autonomously and within a run time limit.
  </p>

  <p>
    The constraint that shapes the software is the platform. There is no
    operating system, no threads, and not enough headroom to poll sensors in a
    main loop while also running motion. Anything that blocks stops the whole
    robot.
  </p>

  <Figure id="walle-field" eager />

  <h2>Role</h2>

  <p>
    I was the sole software developer on a three-person team. I owned the state
    machine architecture, all sensor integration and filtering, and the drive
    routines. My teammates owned the mechanical design, the chassis fabrication
    and the gripper. Where their work is mentioned below it is because it changed
    a software problem.
  </p>

  <h2>Requirements decomposition</h2>

  <DataTable
    caption="Requirements and the mechanism that satisfies each"
    columns={[{ head: 'Requirement' }, { head: 'Mechanism' }]}
    rows={[
      ['Traverse between zones without odometry', 'reflective infrared line following along taped paths'],
      ['Stop at a repeatable distance from a block', 'frontal ultrasonic proximity with hysteresis'],
      ['Classify block color reliably', 'fixed mechanical standoff plus threshold classification with confirmation'],
      ['Ignore decoy blocks', 'stateless skip, no flag retained'],
      ['Never block on a sensor', 'services post discrete events into a shared queue'],
      ['Reorder or add a field zone cheaply', 'identical acquisition sub-machine per zone'],
    ]}
  />

  <h2>Design decisions</h2>

  <h3>One acquisition sub-machine, parameterized per zone</h3>

  <p>
    The top-level state machine sequences four phases: three zone-acquisition
    phases and a sort phase, terminating after the final zone. Each zone phase
    delegates to a sub-machine implementing one consistent acquisition strategy:
    advance under line-sensor supervision, halt at the calibrated sensing
    distance, classify the block, then either initiate a grab and report the
    color upward, or skip and continue.
  </p>

  <p>
    The alternative was a bespoke machine per zone, which is what the field
    layout naturally suggests since the three zones are reached differently. That
    lost on maintenance cost: three bespoke machines means a behavior fix has to
    be applied three times and can be applied inconsistently twice. Because the
    zone sub-machines are the same machine with different parameters, reordering
    the field or adding a zone is an edit to the top-level sequence, and the
    behavior inside a zone is written once.
  </p>

  <p>
    The sort phase dispatches on the reported color into two symmetric flows
    that turn toward the correct drop zone, release, and realign to the line
    before handing control back for the next stage.
  </p>

  <h3>Event-driven sensing, not polled</h3>

  <p>
    Line detection and color classification each run as services that post
    discrete events into a shared queue, so the state machines never poll a
    sensor and never block on one. On a platform with no scheduler this is what
    makes concurrent sensing and motion possible at all, and it is what allows a
    settling interval to be expressed as a timer event rather than a delay loop.
  </p>

  <h3>Decoys are handled statelessly</h3>

  <p>
    A decoy detection transitions the machine onward with no flag and no memory
    of having seen it. The alternative, tracking which decoys had been
    encountered, buys nothing: nothing downstream ever needs to know. Not
    tracking it removes an entire class of latch-up state, where the machine
    holds a flag it can never clear and refuses to re-enter a zone, at zero cost
    to behavior.
  </p>

  <h2>Sensing work</h2>

  <h3>Rangefinder characterization replaced the navigation approach</h3>

  <p>
    The original concept swept an ultrasonic rangefinder across the field to
    localize randomly scattered blocks, which is the obvious use of the only
    ranging sensor in the kit. Bench characterization showed the physical limit
    clearly: the sensor returns accurate range only when the target lies near its
    acoustic axis. During a pivoting sweep the wavefront strikes objects at
    oblique angles and scatters away from the receiver, so detection collapses
    outside a narrow frontal cone and the returns that do arrive are not
    trustworthy bearings.
  </p>

  <p>
    The choice at that point was to filter around the sensor or to stop asking it
    for a measurement it cannot make. I rebuilt navigation around reflective
    infrared line following along taped paths, which turns localization into a
    sequence of discrete line events with no bearing estimate needed at all. The
    rangefinder stayed in the system for what it does well, frontal proximity to
    a block during the approach, where the target is on-axis by construction, and
    came out of the localization role entirely. Its readings there are low-pass
    filtered with hysteresis so a noisy sample cannot bounce the machine between
    approach states.
  </p>

  <FigurePair ids={['walle-hsm-whiteboard', 'walle-hsm-final']} />

  <p>
    Those two diagrams are the clearest artifact on this project. The first is
    the architecture as planned, organized around a search sub-machine doing left
    and right sweeps. The second is the architecture that shipped, where the
    equivalent states are driven by line events instead. The result of the sensor
    study is visible as a structural change, not as a parameter change.
  </p>

  <h3>Color classification, constrained mechanically</h3>

  <p>
    The color sensor was strongly sensitive to both standoff distance and
    ambient lighting, and no threshold set survived both varying together. The
    fix was split across domains rather than solved entirely in software: the
    chassis geometry was changed so a block is always presented at a fixed short
    standoff inside the calibrated range, which removed one of the two variables
    outright. Thresholds were then tuned on empirical data collected under
    consistent lighting.
  </p>

  <p>
    Two guards make the classification commit only when it should. A reading must
    show a minimum separation between channels before it counts as a color at
    all, which rejects ambiguous readings rather than forcing them into the
    nearest class, and a classification must repeat across consecutive samples
    before the event is posted. Constraining the mechanical problem is what made
    the software problem tractable.
  </p>

  <Figure id="walle-cad" />

  <h3>Drivetrain asymmetry</h3>

  <p>
    The two drive motors were measurably unmatched, producing heading drift on
    straight runs and error on turns. I characterized the drift through
    controlled runs and compensated it with per-direction drive-level offsets in
    the motion routines, which held alignment well enough for reliable line
    acquisition. The honest upgrade path is encoder feedback in place of
    hand-characterized compensation: the offsets are calibrated to these two
    motors at this battery voltage and do not transfer.
  </p>

  <h3>Event pacing</h3>

  <p>
    Direction changes introduce mechanical settling transients that corrupt both
    line and color readings if the next event is processed immediately. I
    introduced settling intervals between transitions and sensitive reads and
    tuned them against the platform: too short and the machine decides on a
    reading taken mid-transient, too long and the run time budget suffers.
  </p>

  <Figure id="walle-wiring" />

  <h2>Mechanical context</h2>

  <p>
    A 200 by 200 mm laser-cut tab-and-slot plywood chassis, with 3D printing
    reserved for functional parts only and held to 14.244 in3 against a 30 in3
    budget. My teammates owned this work. The one consequence that reaches the
    software is the gripper standoff geometry described above, which is what made
    color classification repeatable.
  </p>

  <Figure id="walle-drawing" />
</CaseStudy>
